<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Emmeram's Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Emmeram's Abbey, Schloss St. Emmeram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Emmeram's Abbey in Regensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, was a Benedictine monastery founded in the 8th century over the grave of the martyred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Emmeram. For a thousand years it was a leading centre of learning and book production. After secularisation its buildings became in 1812 the residence of the princes of Thurn and Taxis, who still live there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-emmerams-abbey.jpg" alt="St Emmeram's Abbey" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Bernd Gross · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_St._Emmeram_Regensburg_32.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
